\chapter{Methodology}

This chapter describes how the research was designed and executed. It covers
the overall research design, the requirements analysis process, the
user-centred design methodology, the software development approach, and the
evaluation protocol.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Research Design}

This research adopts a \emph{design science} paradigm~\cite{sommerville2016},
in which the primary contribution is an artefact---the H5P interactive video
platform---that is created, described, and evaluated. Design science research
is appropriate for projects that aim to solve a concrete problem for a
defined stakeholder group (university instructors at VNU-HCM) by building
and evaluating a purposeful artefact (a web-based authoring system).

The research combines two methodological phases:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Constructive phase.} User requirements are elicited,
  the system is designed and implemented, and the AI pipeline is built and
  integrated.

  \item \textbf{Evaluative phase.} A comparative usability study measures
  the artefact's performance against the incumbent WordPress-based H5P
  workflow on four metrics: task completion rate, time-on-task, error rate,
  and SUS score.
\end{enumerate}

An \emph{agile development methodology} was adopted for the constructive
phase, using short iterative cycles of 2--3 weeks each~\cite{beck2001}. Each
cycle produced a testable increment of the platform, and informal
walkthroughs at the end of each cycle informed the next design iteration.
This approach was chosen because the interaction between the AI pipeline and
the H5P authoring workflow could not be fully specified in advance; some
requirements emerged only during implementation.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Requirements Analysis}
\label{sec:requirements}

\subsection{User Research}

Prior to system design, informal structured interviews were conducted with
five university instructors at HCMIU to understand their current H5P
authoring experience, their pain points, and their expectations for an
improved tool. Participants were selected to represent a range of technical
proficiency levels and disciplinary backgrounds (engineering, social
sciences, business). The key themes that emerged from these interviews are
summarised in Table~\ref{tab:pain_points}.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Pain points identified in instructor interviews (n\,=\,5)}
  \label{tab:pain_points}
  \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{lXl}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Theme} & \textbf{Representative quote} & \textbf{Frequency} \\
    \midrule
    Interface complexity & ``I have to click through so many pages just to add one question'' & 5/5 \\
    Fear of losing work  & ``Sometimes the page refreshes and I lose everything'' & 4/5 \\
    Time cost            & ``It takes me an entire afternoon to make a short interactive video'' & 5/5 \\
    No preview           & ``I can only see what the question looks like after I export it'' & 4/5 \\
    No AI assistance     & ``I wish I could tell the system to suggest questions for me'' & 3/5 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

A brief observational session in which instructors were asked to create a
simple three-question interactive video using the WordPress H5P plugin was
also conducted. This session confirmed the 45-minute average task completion
time reported in the project brief and revealed two additional insights:
(a)~instructors frequently lost their position in the video while navigating
to and from the question editor, requiring them to re-seek the correct
timestamp; and (b)~instructors with lower technical proficiency abandoned
the task entirely after encountering two consecutive error screens.

Figure~\ref{fig:usecase} presents the complete set of system use cases for
both the Instructor and Admin actors, derived from the user research and
project objectives.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \diagrambox{\textbf{Use Case Overview}\\[6pt]
  Instructor: upload video, edit metadata, request AI suggestions, review
  interactions, export H5P package.\\[4pt]
  Admin: manage users, monitor content, review platform settings.}
  \caption{Use case diagram showing Instructor and Admin actors and their
           interactions with the AI-ActivEdu platform}
  \label{fig:usecase}
\end{figure}

