\subsection{Non-Functional Requirements}

Non-functional requirements govern the quality attributes of the system
and are listed in Table~\ref{tab:nonfunc_reqs}.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Non-functional requirements}
  \label{tab:nonfunc_reqs}
  \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{llX}
    \toprule
    \textbf{ID} & \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Requirement} \\
    \midrule
    NFR-01 & Performance  & REST API responses must complete within 500\,ms for all non-AI endpoints at 50 concurrent users. \\
    NFR-02 & Performance  & Video upload processing (thumbnail generation, metadata extraction) must complete within 30\,s for files $\leq 100$\,MB. \\
    NFR-03 & Security     & All passwords stored as bcrypt hashes with cost factor~$\geq$~12. \\
    NFR-04 & Security     & Authentication tokens must be JWT, signed with HS256, expiring after 7~days. \\
    NFR-05 & Security     & All HTTP responses must include Helmet-generated security headers. \\
    NFR-06 & Security     & All API endpoints must validate and sanitise input before processing. \\
    NFR-07 & Reliability  & The system must handle the absence of both Claude and Ollama gracefully, returning an informative error rather than crashing. \\
    NFR-08 & Usability    & The platform must achieve a SUS score~$\geq$~70 in user testing. \\
    NFR-09 & Usability    & First-time task completion rate for creating a 3-question interactive video must exceed 80\%. \\
    NFR-10 & Compatibility & Exported \texttt{.h5p} packages must be importable into Moodle~4.x and Canvas without modification. \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{User-Centred Design Process}

\section{User-Centred Design Process}
The interface was designed through a three-stage user-centred process: (1)~Conceptual design translating pain points and requirements into user flow diagrams for two primary use cases (manual authoring and AI-assisted workflows), reviewed against Nielsen's heuristics to identify extraneous cognitive load; (2)~Wireframing with heuristic evaluation addressing visibility of system status, error prevention, user control, and recognition-over-recall principles; and (3)~Prototype testing with two IT students using think-aloud protocol, which identified three usability problems (missing timestamp auto-population, hidden status messages, unclear button label) that were resolved before formal evaluation. The redesigned workflow reduced interaction steps from 47 (WordPress baseline) to 8 (new platform), eliminating inter-screen context switching.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Development Methodology}

\subsection{Technology Selection}

Technology selection was guided by four criteria: community maturity,
alignment with the H5P server library's Node.js requirement, developer
familiarity, and suitability for the AI pipeline.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Frontend: React~18 + TypeScript + Vite.}
  React was selected because it is the most widely adopted JavaScript UI
  framework, providing a large ecosystem of compatible libraries and
  extensive documentation~\cite{react2023}. TypeScript was mandatory because
  the AI pipeline involves multiple JSON schemas (transcript segments,
  AI suggestions, H5P content parameters) that must be kept consistent
  across client and server~\cite{typescript2023}. Vite was chosen as the
  build tool for its sub-second hot module replacement~\cite{vite2023}.

  \item \textbf{Backend: Node.js + Express.js.}
  Node.js was selected to maintain a single language across the stack and to
  satisfy the \texttt{@lumieducation/h5p-server} dependency~\cite{nodejs2023,
  expressjs2023}. Express.js provides a minimal, middleware-based HTTP
  framework that integrates cleanly with the H5P server library.

  \item \textbf{Database: SQLite via Sequelize ORM.}
  SQLite was selected for its zero-configuration deployment and its
  sufficiency for the expected data volumes (hundreds of users, thousands
  of videos)~\cite{sqlite2023}. The Sequelize ORM provides a type-safe
  model layer, migration support, and future flexibility to switch to
  PostgreSQL if institutional scale demands it~\cite{sequelize2023}.

  \item \textbf{AI: Anthropic Claude + Ollama.}
  Anthropic Claude was selected as the cloud AI provider because of its
  strong instruction-following and reliable JSON output~\cite{anthropic2024}.
  Ollama was added as a first-choice local provider to enable cost-free,
  privacy-preserving operation in environments where API access is
  unavailable~\cite{brown2020}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Development Cycles}

The project was executed in five development cycles, each 2--3 weeks long,
over a twelve-week period. Table~\ref{tab:dev_cycles} summarises the scope
of each cycle.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Development cycle summary}
  \label{tab:dev_cycles}
  \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{llX}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Cycle} & \textbf{Weeks} & \textbf{Deliverables} \\
    \midrule
    1 & 1--2  & Project setup, database schema, user authentication (JWT + bcrypt), basic video upload. \\
    2 & 3--5  & H5P service integration, manual question editor, real-time preview. \\
    3 & 6--8  & YouTube import, transcript extraction, AI analysis service (Claude + Ollama), SSE streaming. \\
    4 & 9--10 & AI suggestion review panel, H5P injection service, export functionality. \\
    5 & 11--12 & Dashboard enhancements, security hardening (Helmet, rate limiting, Zod validation), admin panel, LTI endpoint. \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Evaluation Methodology}

The evaluation employed a within-subject comparative usability study with ten IT students at HCMIU, who completed identical authoring tasks on both the new platform and WordPress H5P plugin in counterbalanced order, with 30-minute rest between sessions. Participants (mean age 21.3 years; 6 male, 4 female; all novices to H5P, 8/10 with minimal WordPress experience, 7/10 with basic web development knowledge) served as educator proxies per standard usability evaluation practice. Task scenarios consisted of: (1)~upload a 5-minute video, (2)~add $\geq 3$ H5P questions at appropriate timestamps, and (3)~export the H5P package; on the new platform, participants additionally (4)~reviewed AI-generated suggestions. Four primary metrics were collected: Task Completion Rate (percentage completing all sub-tasks within 20 minutes), Time-on-Task (seconds from start to valid .h5p package export), Error Count (observable mistakes, correction requirements, or dead-ends), and System Usability Scale (SUS $> 68$ above average, >80 excellent). Qualitative data came from think-aloud verbalisations during sessions and semi-structured interviews afterward. The baseline system was WordPress H5P plugin v1.15.8 on standard LAMP stack, with 5-minute orientation equivalent to university ICT induction.
